# Per-package statement-coverage floors, enforced by scripts/coverage-gate.sh in CI.
#
# Each floor is that package's measured coverage rounded down to the nearest whole percent, so
# this file is green on the commit that introduces it. That is the point: a gate set above where
# the code is gets weakened until it passes, and a gate set at one number across a repo with a
# real spread has to sit at the floor of the worst package, at which point it gates nothing.
#
# Every line is a ratchet. Raising a floor is a deliberate act. Lowering one has to be defended
# in a diff, which is the only property that makes this file worth having — the alternative is a
# coverage number that drifts down a percent at a time and is never anyone's fault.
#
# CLAUDE.md's target is 80%+ per package. Sixteen of the thirty-five packages below are already
# there. The gap is concentrated in the packages the refactor is about to rewrite, which is where
# raising a floor before the rewrite would buy tests of code that is being deleted:
#
#   internal/fuse       37.7%  — becomes a thin shim over internal/vfs (task #22)
#   internal/adapter    45.7%  — the write path is being rebuilt around dirty ranges (task #20)
#   internal/health     45.0%  — reachable now that pkg/health's probe path exists
#   pkg/errors          48.3%  — mostly classification tables; needs table tests, not design work
#
# Floors are deliberately absent for four packages with no test files at all. A floor of 0% reads
# as a target rather than as an absence, and the gate names unlisted packages in every run so the
# omission stays visible instead of looking like a decision:
#
#   cmd/objectfs        no tests — argument parsing and signal handling, currently untestable
#                       because main() calls log.Fatalf directly
#   pkg/archive         no tests — metadata types; dead code pending a decision (Part 4)
#   pkg/compression     no tests in this package; its constants and SupportedAlgorithms list are
#                       exercised from internal/compression, which is at 87.3%
#   sdks/c              no tests — cgo shim, not reachable from `go test`

